    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80806.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, Ryan, I watched almost all of it remotely and it felt like a Trump rally on decaf, just very low energy, a little bit unfocused, no prompter. A message meant to be focused on immigration and migrant crime, Donald Trump's two kind of pet issues. But I don't know that it did a lot for them. The theory behind it was the idea of getting out early, making sure that he could kind of set the tone for news coverage for the day. ahead of the vice president's remarks later this evening, adding something MR. Lago, they know they can get news coverage, they know they can get national crews down there. But I don't know that whatever this was supposed to be for the Trump campaign, it was very effective in doing that. What I think most people took away from it, what I took away from it. was his complaints about what he started to seed the ground of bad things happening in Pennsylvania suggesting there might be cheating going on in the election up here. To me, that was probably the single biggest takeaway from that whole event. I don't know that anything moved the needle with voters who might still be making decisions out there.",
