    "turn_idx": 51,
    "turn_text": "All right, Michael Tyler, thank you. I know you had to fight through some background noise there to get through this interview. You did a yeoman's effort to make that happen. We appreciate it. And good luck the rest of the way on this campaign. Up next, election security concerns in a key house race as authorities search for a suspect tied to a pair of ballot box fires, including one that damaged hundreds of ballots in the swing district. The incumbent House member in that district joins me next. You're watching Meet the Press Now. Welcome back now, turning to the latest in the investigation tied to a pair of ballot box fires out West. Officials say that an incendiary device was found in a ballot box in Portland, Oregon, and a ballot box fire in neighboring Vancouver, Washington that damaged hundreds of ballots, and they are likely connected. An identical Volvo was spotted at both scenes, and the use of that incendiary device in Portland was described as similar in nature to what happened in Vancouver. Democratic Congresswoman Marie Loosen Camp Perez, who represents the Vancouver, Washington area, is calling for round-the-clock law enforcement presence at all ballot drop boxes locations in that county. Loosen Camp Perez flipped her seat from red to blue in 2022 and has now won a five-house Democrat seeking reelection in a district that Trump won in 2020. She's in a tough race against Trump-backed Republican Joe Kent, and I see that could be key to which party controls Congress next year. And the Congresswoman joins us now to talk more about everything that is happening in her district. Congresswoman, thank you so much for being here. First off, have you received a response from authorities about your request for round-the-clock presences at these ballot drop boxes? Is that going to happen?",
